    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cunedda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cunedda.jpg" alt="Cunedda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cunedda">Cunedda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crop_Cunedda_from_File_History_of_the_Kings.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Gwynedd took shape in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in the generations after Roman rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> ended early in the fifth century, in a region that had been guarded by the Roman fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segontium">Segontium</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caernarfon">Caernarfon</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name, Venedotia, appears in an early form on a sixth-century gravestone at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penmachno">Penmachno</a>. Medieval tradition, first recorded in the ninth-century <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a></em>, held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunedda">Cunedda</a> came with his sons from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manaw-gododdin">Manaw Gododdin</a>, the district around the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a>, and expelled Irish settlers from Wales; many historians doubt the story or its date, but the later kings of Gwynedd all traced their line to him. The first ruler known from a near-contemporary source is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maelgwn-gwynedd">Maelgwn Gwynedd</a>, whom the British writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gildas">Gildas</a>, probably in the first half of the sixth century, denounced as Maglocunus, the ‘dragon of the island’, a powerful king who had overthrown rivals and broken monastic vows. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annales-cambriae">Annales Cambriae</a></em> place Maelgwn’s death in 547, during a great plague. The kingdom’s heartland was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a>, Eryri, a natural fortress of mountains, together with the fertile island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>, Môn, whose grain fed the court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberffraw">Aberffraw</a> on Anglesey became its chief royal seat.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dublin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dublin.jpg" alt="Dublin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dublin_-_aerial_-_2025-07-07_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">瑞丽江的河水, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gwynedd was a land of mountain pasture and narrow coastal plains, and its economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep and the cereals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>, praised in a later saying as <em>Môn mam Cymru</em>, ‘Anglesey, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>’, because its harvests could feed the mainland. Society was ordered by kinship and status. Free men held hereditary shares of land within their kindred and owed the king food renders, hospitality and military service, while bond tenants in royal townships, the <em>maerdrefi</em>, worked the demesne land attached to each royal court. Welsh law fixed compensation for injury and killing and divided land equally among sons. Trade was modest: the kingdom exported cattle, hides and wool and imported wine, salt and cloth, much of it through contacts across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a>, especially with Hiberno-Norse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, where Gruffudd ap Cynan was born and raised. The church was rooted in early monastic foundations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangor-abbey">Bangor</a>, traditionally founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-deiniol">Saint Deiniol</a> in the sixth century, became the seat of the diocese, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clynnog-fawr">Clynnog Fawr</a>, associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-beuno">Saint Beuno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penmon-priory">Penmon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holyhead">Holyhead</a> were important churches. The thirteenth-century princes favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cistercians">Cistercians</a>, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberconwy-abbey">Aberconwy Abbey</a>, founded in 1186, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-the-great">Llywelyn the Great</a> was buried. Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llys-rhosyr">Llys Rhosyr</a> on Anglesey have uncovered the remains of one of the royal courts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase.jpg" alt="Battle of Hatfield Chase" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase">Battle of Hatfield Chase</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Death_of_Edwin_of_Northumbria_at_Hatfield_-_F._H._Townsend.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">F. H. Townsend, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The armies of Gwynedd were built around the king’s household troop, the <em>teulu</em>, and the levy of free men, who fought mainly as spearmen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and used the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a> as refuge and fortress. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadwallon-ap-cadfan">Cadwallon ap Cadfan</a>, in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a>, defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-of-northumbria">Edwin of Northumbria</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase">Battle of Hatfield Chase</a> in 633 and ravaged Northumbria before he was himself killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-heavenfield">Battle of Heavenfield</a> in 634. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodri-the-great">Rhodri the Great</a> killed the Danish leader Horm in 856, and in 881 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarawd-ap-rhodri">Anarawd</a> defeated the Mercians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-conwy">Conwy</a>, a victory the annals called vengeance for Rhodri. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-ap-llywelyn">Gruffudd ap Llywelyn</a> defeated the Mercians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rhyd-y-groes">Rhyd-y-groes</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">Severn</a> in 1039 and later raided deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-gwynedd">Owain Gwynedd</a> ambushed Henry II’s army in 1157 before coming to terms, and with a coalition of Welsh rulers saw Henry’s great invasion of 1165 founder in rain on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berwyn-range">Berwyn</a> mountains. The thirteenth-century princes built stone castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolwyddelan-castle">Dolwyddelan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criccieth">Criccieth</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castell-y-bere">Castell y Bere</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolforwyn-castle">Dolforwyn</a>. Yet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I</a>’s campaigns of 1277 and 1282–1283 showed the limits of Welsh power: English armies, supplied by sea, seized the harvest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a> and slowly encircled Snowdonia, despite a Welsh victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-moel-y-don">Moel-y-don</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menai-strait">Menai Strait</a> in November 1282.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hywel-dda.jpg" alt="Hywel Dda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hyweldda_historieofcambria.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Humphrey Llwyd, David Powell, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gwynedd’s kings, and from the twelfth century its princes, ruled through a network of courts, the <em>llysoedd</em>, one in each commote, or <em>cwmwd</em>, the subdivision of the larger cantref. Each court was the centre of a district in which a <em>maer</em> managed the royal lands and bond tenants and a <em>rhaglaw</em> exercised the ruler’s authority over free men, while the royal household moved between courts to consume their food renders. Succession was open to any male of the royal kin, which produced repeated wars between brothers and cousins. The kingdom’s law was written down in the thirteenth century in the version known as <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llyfr-iorwerth">Llyfr Iorwerth</a></em>, the Gwynedd redaction of the laws attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-gwynedd">Owain Gwynedd</a> (r. 1137–1170), who styled himself prince of the Welsh, and Llywelyn ab Iorwerth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-the-great">Llywelyn the Great</a> (r. c. 1195–1240), government became more formal. Llywelyn issued charters, kept a household of officials led by his steward, the <em>distain</em> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ednyfed-fychan">Ednyfed Fychan</a>, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-countess-of-toulouse">Joan</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-worcester">Treaty of Worcester</a> of 1218 obtained recognition of his conquests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> went further, levying heavy taxes and winning, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-montgomery">Treaty of Montgomery</a> of 1267, recognition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry III</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> with the homage of the other Welsh lords.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/beaumaris-castle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beaumaris-castle.jpg" alt="Beaumaris Castle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/beaumaris-castle">Beaumaris Castle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castell_Beaumaris_Castle,_Ynys_Mon_(Anglesey),_Wales_45.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Llywelyn2000, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gwynedd became the leading Welsh kingdom and, under its last princes, the core of an attempt to unite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> as a single principality. The line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodri-the-great">Rhodri the Great</a>, through his sons and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a>, supplied kings to much of Wales, and the later dynasties of Deheubarth and Gwynedd both descended from him. The kingdom’s early Christian culture left monuments such as the stone at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llangadwaladr">Llangadwaladr</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a> commemorating Cadfan ap Iago as the ‘wisest and most renowned of all kings’. From the twelfth century the princes’ courts sustained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poets-of-the-princes">Poets of the Princes</a>, the <em>Gogynfeirdd</em>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meilyr-brydydd">Meilyr Brydydd</a>, Gwalchmai ap Meilyr and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywarch-ap-llywelyn">Llywarch ap Llywelyn</a>, known as Prydydd y Moch, whose elaborate praise poetry is a major body of medieval Welsh literature, and the life of Gruffudd ap Cynan, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and preserved also in Welsh, is a rare secular biography of a Welsh king. The idea of a principality of Wales outlived its creators: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I</a> kept the title and gave it to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-ii-of-england">Edward of Caernarfon</a> in 1301, and it has been held by heirs to the English and British throne since. Edward’s castles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caernarfon">Caernarfon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-conwy">Conwy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaumaris-castle">Beaumaris</a> now form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. North-west Wales remains the region where Welsh is most widely spoken, and the modern county of Gwynedd revives the old name.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rhodri-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhodri-the-great.jpg" alt="Rhodri the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rhodri-the-great">Rhodri the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhodri_Mawr_-_Historiae_Cambriae.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David Powell, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The power of Gwynedd rose and fell with its dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodri-the-great">Rhodri the Great</a>’s lands were divided after his death in 878, and in 942 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a> of Deheubarth took over Gwynedd; through the tenth century the kingdom suffered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a> and wars between rival claimants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-ap-llywelyn">Gruffudd ap Llywelyn</a> reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> from Gwynedd but was killed in 1063 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a>’s campaign, and from about 1081 Norman lords from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> occupied much of the kingdom until a Welsh revolt in 1094 and the return of Gruffudd ap Cynan. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-the-great">Llywelyn the Great</a> died in 1240, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry III</a> cut back his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dafydd-ap-gruffydd">Dafydd</a>’s power, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-woodstock">Treaty of Woodstock</a> of 1247 confined Dafydd’s heirs to the lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-conwy">River Conwy</a>. The final crisis came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a>, who after 1272 refused homage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I</a> and fell behind on the payments owed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-montgomery">Treaty of Montgomery</a>. The war of 1277 ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-aberconwy">Treaty of Aberconwy</a>, which again confined him to Gwynedd west of the Conwy. In 1282 his brother Dafydd ap Gruffudd attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawarden-castle">Hawarden Castle</a>, and Llywelyn joined the rising; he was killed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilmeri">Cilmeri</a> on 11 December 1282. Dafydd was captured in June 1283 and executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a> that October. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-rhuddlan">Statute of Rhuddlan</a> of 1284 divided Gwynedd into the shires of Anglesey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caernarfon">Caernarfon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merionethshire">Merioneth</a> under English officials.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Gwynedd was the most durable of the Welsh kingdoms, rooted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>, whose rulers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maelgwn-gwynedd">Maelgwn Gwynedd</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> repeatedly led native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, created the idea of a Welsh principality and sustained a flowering of court poetry before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I</a> conquered it in 1282–1283.</em></p>

